# Enforce LF for all text so a clone on ANY OS reproduces the exact bytes the server hashed.
# The deployed codeHash (/build, proof.js buildId) is computed over LF source. Without this, a Windows
# checkout with core.autocrlf=true rewrites .js line endings to CRLF, the recomputed buildId diverges
# from /build, and that falsely reads as "live code != repo" — the worst possible false alarm for a
# product whose whole claim is verifiability. See REPRODUCIBLE.md.
* text=auto eol=lf
*.js    text eol=lf
*.mjs   text eol=lf
*.cjs   text eol=lf
*.ts    text eol=lf
*.json  text eol=lf
*.md    text eol=lf
*.html  text eol=lf
*.css   text eol=lf
*.yml   text eol=lf
*.yaml  text eol=lf
*.sh    text eol=lf
# .sql is in the research manifest whose hashes a reader recomputes from a clone. It was relying on
# `text=auto` sniffing, and one query file sat in the worktree with a stray CRLF while git stored LF —
# invisible to `git status`, which compares post-normalisation, and fatal to a published sha256.
*.sql   text eol=lf
# .csv joins it for the same reason: research/BUYER_LEDGER.csv is now a published artifact and
# Section 6.4 tells a reader to recompute its figures from a clone. It was normalising only through
# the `text=auto` catch-all above — which is exactly what .sql was relying on when a stray CRLF made
# a published hash unreproducible. Explicit is the whole lesson.
*.csv   text eol=lf

# Binary assets — never normalize.
*.png  binary
*.jpg  binary
*.jpeg binary
*.gif  binary
*.ico  binary
*.pdf  binary
*.woff binary
*.woff2 binary
